WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
Film Courage: Brad can you take us through how&nbsp;
you lit certain scenes for A California Christmas?

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.400
Brad Rushing, Cinematographer: We have this one&nbsp;
here where Lauren [Writer/Actress Lauren Swickard]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:13.760
is on the phone. That's a scene Cali's bedroom.&nbsp;
I remember it's on the second floor and when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.600
Shaun [Director Shaun Paul Piccinino] said he&nbsp;
wanted to shoot in that bedroom I was like Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:24.480
I’m going to have to get an extra high stand.&nbsp;
So we specifically ordered an Avenger stand for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:31.040
this shot and then also there's a scene in mom's&nbsp;
bedroom where she's sick and she's bed bound. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:36.400
one also we needed that Avenger stand because a&nbsp;
normal stand would not get us up to an altitude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:42.400
where we could get a good angle like we'd be&nbsp;
pointing up, so that's a special stand. We shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:50.960
it in the day with an M18 which is the 1800 Watt&nbsp;
HMI Par. I remember in that moment worrying that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:56.400
there was a little too much glare and even when we&nbsp;
did the color grade I was like Shaun, you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:03.680
make that go away a little bit he's like Oh,&nbsp;
no I love it. He was right, it's beautiful but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:09.680
I'm just it's glare is just one of those things&nbsp;
you know just like a flare you know it it can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:16.560
aesthetically pleasing or it can be you know&nbsp;
an obnoxious artifact and and it's just a fine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.840
line and it's it's really nice in in this scene&nbsp;
that was one thing I thought about and but this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:29.440
is a very simply lit scene I mean it was I think&nbsp;
we may have put some a negative fill behind the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:34.320
camera you know put some black up just to put a&nbsp;
little bit of contrast in it because that light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:40.560
was bouncing off of those white walls but not&nbsp;
a lot not a lot going on there we just kept it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:45.840
simple and and let that window do its thing we&nbsp;
also we we pick her up with a camera move that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:51.200
a pan that we pick her up coming in the door and&nbsp;
then we follow her over to where she's standing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:56.640
in front of that window so that's a a nice&nbsp;
transition too and I think that was the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:02.720
shot we did I don't know if we did two sizes&nbsp;
of that if we did it was only two sizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:08.160
not a lot of coverage that was one of those scenes&nbsp;
we shot simple and quick and and she just you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:15.440
she just nailed it so here we have the barn scene&nbsp;
yeah I remember when we were scouting this angle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:23.920
and Shaun saw that I mean behind us is is kind of&nbsp;
just a blind little cubicle almost like a stall&nbsp;&nbsp;

